IMPORT prelude
-- SUM-TYPE-FIELDS-SPEC §3 S3: narrowing is a CHECK-TIME fact.
--
-- `monthly_rent` is declared by `Tenant` alone, so reading it off a value that
-- could still be a `Landlord` is refused by §3 S2 — that refusal is pinned by
-- `not-ok/tc/partial-projection.l4`. THIS file is the other side: four ways of
-- telling the checker the value is a `Tenant`, each of which makes the very
-- same read legal.
--
-- Every one of them is ARMED — `#EVAL` or `#TRACE` — because a narrowing that
-- only satisfies the checker is worth nothing: the point is that the read is
-- also SAFE, and before §3 S2 the un-narrowed form died at run time with a
-- message about a CONSIDER the drafter never wrote (§1.1).
--
-- The quantifier case in particular is the ONLY test of that path anywhere in
-- the tree: §4 measured that every existing `every/` filter narrows with `elem`
-- or `EQUALS` and none with a narrowing constructor.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord HAS deposit      IS A NUMBER
    Tenant   HAS monthly_rent IS A NUMBER

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign HAS signer IS AN Actor

theLandlord MEANS Landlord OF 4000
alice       MEANS Tenant OF 1500
bob         MEANS Tenant OF 900
everyone    MEANS LIST theLandlord, alice, bob

-- 1. THE NARROWING CONSTRUCTOR. `EVERY Tenant a` says the members are
--    `Tenant`s, so `a's monthly_rent` in the WHO filter is total. The roll
--    filter drops `theLandlord` before the filter ever sees it, which is what
--    the checker has now learned to agree with.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`rich tenants sign` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant a IN everyone
        WHO    a's monthly_rent AT LEAST 1000
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY a)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `rich tenants sign` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1

-- 2. A `WHEN` BRANCH NARROWS THE SCRUTINEE. The branch binds `t` to the
--    PAYLOAD, not to the value; what it narrows is `a`, the name it is
--    scrutinising. So `a's monthly_rent` is legal inside the branch and
--    nowhere else in this function.
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rent via when` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN Landlord d THEN 0
    WHEN Tenant t   THEN a's monthly_rent

#EVAL `rent via when` alice
#EVAL `rent via when` theLandlord

-- 3. AN `OTHERWISE` GETS THE RESIDUAL. `Landlord` is consumed by the branch
--    above it — `WHEN Landlord d` is irrefutable, every sub-pattern a plain
--    variable — so the only constructor left to reach the OTHERWISE is
--    `Tenant`, and the read is total there.
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rent via otherwise` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN Landlord d THEN 0
    OTHERWISE a's monthly_rent

#EVAL `rent via otherwise` alice
#EVAL `rent via otherwise` theLandlord

-- 4. AN ALIAS SEES THROUGH TO WHAT IT NAMES. `b MEANS a` is not a new value,
--    so `b` is narrowed exactly where `a` is. This is what stops naming a
--    subexpression from changing whether the program checks (§3.1).
GIVEN a IS AN Actor
GIVETH A NUMBER
`rent via alias` a MEANS
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN Landlord d THEN 0
    OTHERWISE b's monthly_rent
    WHERE
        b MEANS a

#EVAL `rent via alias` alice
#EVAL `rent via alias` theLandlord

-- 4b. …and through a chain that ends at a CONSTRUCTION. `alice` is written as
--     `Tenant OF 1500` and L4 has no mutation, so it denotes that construction
--     unconditionally and the read needs no CONSIDER at all.
#EVAL alice's monthly_rent
#EVAL theLandlord's deposit

-- A nullary constructor is statically itself, so a field of a DIFFERENT arm is
-- still refused there — see `not-ok/tc/partial-projection.l4`. Nothing in this
-- file relies on that; it is named so a reader does not assume 4b is a blanket
-- permission.
